Theory
Logistic Regression is one of the most widely used supervised machine learning algorithms
for solving classification problems. Unlike Linear Regression, which predicts continuous
values, Logistic Regression predicts the probability that a given input belongs to a particular
class. It is primarily used for binary classification problems where the output has only two
possible values, such as Yes/No, True/False, or 0/1.
The algorithm uses a mathematical function called the Sigmoid Function to transform the
output of a linear equation into a probability value between 0 and 1. Based on a predefined
threshold (usually 0.5), the predicted probability is converted into a class label. If the
predicted probability is greater than or equal to 0.5, the observation is classified into one
class; otherwise, it is assigned to the other class.
Logistic Regression estimates the relationship between one or more independent variables
and a categorical dependent variable. The model parameters are estimated using the
Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE) method instead of the Least Squares method used
in Linear Regression.
Although Logistic Regression is a simple algorithm, it provides high accuracy for linearly
separable datasets and serves as a strong baseline for many classification tasks. It is
computationally efficient, easy to interpret, and widely used in industry and research.
