Theory
Ensemble Learning is a machine learning technique in which multiple models are combined
to produce a better predictive model than any individual model. Instead of relying on a
single classifier or regressor, Ensemble Learning aggregates the predictions of several weak
or strong learners to improve overall accuracy, reduce overfitting, and increase model
stability.
The basic idea behind Ensemble Learning is that different models make different errors.
When these models are combined intelligently, their errors tend to cancel out, leading to
improved performance.
There are two major Ensemble Learning techniques:
1. Bagging (Bootstrap Aggregating)
Bagging involves creating multiple subsets of the original training dataset using random
sampling with replacement. Each subset is used to train an independent model. The final
prediction is obtained by majority voting (classification) or averaging (regression).
Random Forest is the most popular Bagging algorithm.
2. Boosting
Boosting builds models sequentially. Each new model focuses on correcting the mistakes
made by previous models. It assigns higher importance to misclassified samples and
gradually improves prediction accuracy.
Popular Boosting algorithms include:
● AdaBoost
● Gradient Boosting
● XGBoost
Compared to Bagging, Boosting generally achieves higher accuracy but requires more
computation.
