Theory
Support Vector Machine (SVM) is a supervised machine learning algorithm used for both
classification and regression tasks. It is primarily employed for classification problems
where the objective is to find an optimal decision boundary that separates different classes
with the maximum possible margin.
The boundary created by SVM is known as a hyperplane. The data points closest to the
hyperplane are called support vectors, and they play a significant role in determining the
position of the decision boundary.
When data cannot be separated linearly, SVM uses kernel functions to transform the data
into a higher-dimensional space where linear separation becomes possible.
Common kernel functions include:
● Linear Kernel
● Polynomial Kernel
● Radial Basis Function (RBF)
● Sigmoid Kernel
SVM is particularly effective for high-dimensional datasets and is widely used in image
classification, text classification, and medical diagnosis.
