● Improve visualization of high-dimensional data.
● Compare supervised and unsupervised dimensionality reduction techniques.
Theory
In many real-world machine learning applications, datasets contain a large number of
features. High-dimensional datasets increase computational complexity, consume more
memory, and may reduce model performance due to redundant or irrelevant information.
Dimensionality reduction techniques help overcome these problems by transforming the
original dataset into a smaller set of meaningful features while preserving most of the
important information.
Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is an unsupervised dimensionality reduction
technique. It transforms the original features into a new set of orthogonal variables called
principal components. These components capture the maximum variance present in the
data, allowing fewer dimensions to represent most of the information. PCA is widely used
for visualization, compression, and feature extraction.
Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) is a matrix factorization technique that decomposes
a matrix into three smaller matrices. It is extensively used in recommendation systems,
image compression, natural language processing, and latent semantic analysis. SVD can
efficiently handle sparse and high-dimensional data.
Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA) is a supervised dimensionality reduction technique
that utilizes class labels to maximize the separation between different classes. Unlike PCA,
which focuses on maximizing variance, LDA aims to improve class discrimination, making it
highly effective for classification problems.
