Theory
Graph-Based Clustering is an unsupervised learning technique in which data points are
represented as vertices (nodes) and the similarity between them is represented by edges.
Instead of relying on distance alone, this approach models relationships between data
points, making it suitable for complex datasets with irregular structures.
One commonly used graph-based approach is the Minimum Spanning Tree (MST). In this
method, a graph is constructed connecting all vertices with the minimum possible total edge
weight without forming cycles. By removing edges with high weights, the graph naturally
separates into multiple clusters.
Another widely used approach is Spectral Clustering, which uses the eigenvalues and
eigenvectors of the graph Laplacian matrix to identify clusters. Spectral clustering performs
well on datasets where traditional clustering algorithms struggle.
Graph-Based Clustering is particularly useful for social networks, biological networks,
image segmentation, recommendation systems, and communication networks.
