Theory
DBSCAN (Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise) is an unsupervised
machine learning algorithm used to identify clusters based on the density of data points.
Unlike K-Means clustering, DBSCAN does not require the number of clusters to be specified
before execution. It is capable of discovering clusters of arbitrary shapes while
simultaneously identifying noisy data points as outliers.
The algorithm uses two important parameters: Epsilon (ε), which defines the
neighborhood radius around a data point, and MinPts, which specifies the minimum
number of neighboring points required to form a dense region. Based on these parameters,
data points are classified into Core Points, Border Points, and Noise Points.
DBSCAN starts by selecting an unvisited point and checks whether it satisfies the density
requirement. If the point has enough neighboring points, it becomes a core point and a new
cluster is created. Neighboring points are then recursively added to the cluster. Points that
do not belong to any cluster are marked as noise.
DBSCAN is particularly effective for datasets containing irregular cluster shapes and noisy
observations. It is widely applied in geographic information systems, image processing,
anomaly detection, and market segmentation.
