Theory
Classification and Regression Tree (CART) is a supervised machine learning algorithm used
for both classification and regression problems. It constructs a binary decision tree by
repeatedly splitting the dataset into smaller subsets based on the values of input features.
Each split is selected to maximize the purity of the resulting subsets, making the tree
capable of making accurate predictions.
For classification tasks, CART generally uses the Gini Index to measure the impurity of a
node. The feature producing the maximum reduction in impurity is selected as the splitting
attribute. For regression problems, CART uses Mean Squared Error (MSE) to determine
the best split by minimizing the prediction error.
The tree consists of three major components:
● Root Node: Represents the entire dataset and performs the first split.
● Internal Nodes: Represent decision conditions based on feature values.
● Leaf Nodes: Represent the final prediction or class label.
Decision Trees are simple to understand and interpret because they represent decision-
making in a graphical form. However, very large trees may memorize the training data,
leading to overfitting. To overcome this problem, tree pruning is performed to remove
unnecessary branches and improve generalization.
The CART algorithm is widely used because it requires minimal data preprocessing and can
handle both numerical and categorical features. It is also the base learner for many
powerful ensemble methods such as Random Forest and Gradient Boosting.
