EXPERIMENT 10
Implementation of Linear Discriminant
Analysis (LDA)
Aim
To study and implement Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA) for dimensionality reduction
and classification using Python.
Lab Objectives
● To understand the concept of Linear Discriminant Analysis.
● To reduce the dimensionality of datasets using LDA.
● To maximize class separability for classification.
● To compare LDA with other dimensionality reduction techniques.
Lab Outcomes
After successful completion of this experiment, students will be able to:
● Implement Linear Discriminant Analysis using Python.
● Apply LDA for feature extraction.
● Improve classification performance using reduced features.
● Compare LDA with Principal Component Analysis (PCA).
Theory
Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA) is a supervised dimensionality reduction and
classification technique used to project high-dimensional data into a lower-dimensional
space while preserving the discriminatory information between different classes. Unlike
Principal Component Analysis (PCA), which focuses on maximizing variance, LDA uses class
labels to maximize the separation between different categories.
LDA works by finding a transformation that maximizes the ratio of between-class variance
to within-class variance. This results in a new feature space where data points belonging
to different classes are more distinguishable, thereby improving the performance of
classification algorithms.
The algorithm first computes the mean vectors for each class, followed by the within-class
scatter matrix and the between-class scatter matrix. It then determines the optimal
projection directions by solving an eigenvalue problem. Finally, the original data is
projected onto these directions to obtain a lower-dimensional representation.
LDA is widely used in pattern recognition, facial recognition, medical diagnosis, document
classification, and speech recognition. Since it considers class information during feature
extraction, it generally performs better than PCA when the objective is classification.
Applications
● Face Recognition
● Medical Diagnosis
● Handwriting Recognition
● Speech Recognition
● Text Classification
● Pattern Recognition
● Bioinformatics
Advantages
● Improves class separability.
● Reduces computational complexity.
● Enhances classification accuracy.
● Utilizes class label information.
● Simple and computationally efficient.
Limitations
● Applicable only to labeled datasets.
● Assumes normally distributed data.
● Sensitive to outliers.
● Performance decreases when assumptions are violated.
